2:## The world in a window. Host mode `world-view` (kernel/host/host.gd; N5b patch 4), two uses:
3:##
4:## N5b, pictures of a world of packs (tools/view.sh shot / check):
5:##   <godot> --position 0,1440 --resolution 1920x1080 --rendering-driver d3d12 --fixed-fps 60 --path <engine>
6:##           res://kernel/host/host.tscn -- world-view --packs=<dir>,<dir>,... [--at=x:y:z,..] [--yaw=deg,..]
7:##           [--spacing=80] [--stage=0] [--cam=<view of the stage pack>|overview] [--frame=300] --out=<abs png>
8:##           [--mech=a|b] [--cull=auto|aabb|margin] [--standins=off|auto|all] [--far=150] [--until=end]
9:##           [--also=<stage>:<cam>:<abs png>;..] [--mode=bot|smoke] [--frames=36000] [--seeds=..] [--ground=on|off]
10:##           [--hud=on|off] [--measure=120] [--radius=40,..] [--lift=m,..] [--crane=x:y:z,x:y:z] [--settle=frames] [--band=9]
11:##   The packs are loaded by the world host and stay at the origin of their own physics space: `--at` is the PLOT of
12:##   each pack. Every pack is ticked on every frame. Output: one `tl.view/1` line per picture; with `--until=end` the
13:##   world then runs to its end and prints its `tl.world/1` lines.
14:##
15:## N7, one world you can walk through (tools/world.sh tour / visit):
16:##   ... -- world-view --walk=<walk script> --out-dir=<abs dir> [--log=<file>] [--ticks=N] [--landscape=on|off] [--live]
17:##   The SCHEDULED world (kernel/world/tiers.gd) in a window: the tier of each plot decides its ticks (world.gd) and
18:##   its drawing (here): `stage` = zero offset, its cameras, its HUD; `sideline` = drawn in full at its plot;
19:##   `backdrop` and `ledger` = its stand-in mesh. A tier change is applied at the tick boundary it happens on
20:##   (`plot_event` of the world host). The visitor (kernel/world/visitor.gd) walks the world's landscape
21:##   (kernel/world/landscape.gd); while he walks nobody is on stage. The walk's `shot` events become numbered pictures.
22:##   kernel/world/WORLD.md has the whole picture.
